WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:01.590
You don't know me.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:02.693
How do you know what I'm about

00:00:02.693 --> 00:00:04.639
to say is something you can trust?

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:06.984
It's actually your responsibility to make

00:00:06.984 --> 00:00:09.380
sure that your information is authentic.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:12.229
This is where data literacy comes in.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.769
It can help you evaluate the reliability of news sources.

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:18.800
Make informed decisions about your health and finances.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.590
Even protect you from scams and misinformation.

00:00:22.413 --> 00:00:24.852
In schooling, data literacy can help you understand

00:00:24.852 --> 00:00:29.059
and analyze complex information in subjects like math and science.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:30.752
And in the business world,

00:00:30.752 --> 00:00:32.514
a culture of data literacy means

00:00:32.514 --> 00:00:36.710
everyone in the organization can make better data-driven decisions.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.727
Plus, it's a valuable skill that can open up career

00:00:39.727 --> 00:00:43.219
opportunities in fields like data science and market research.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.919
So don't just consume your data at face value.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:48.082
To learn more about all things data,

00:00:48.082 --> 00:00:49.986
head on over to the Data Differentiator,

00:00:49.986 --> 00:00:53.149
IBM's guide for data leaders and enthusiasts alike.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:54.600
We hope to see you there.

